  <conditions attached="1">
   <condition type="1">
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Consistent with the Article III of the SBAA [or the Supplemental Provisions to the Project Document], the responsibility for the safety and security of the Implementing Partner and its personnel and property, and of UNDP&#x2019;s property in the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s custody, rests with the Implementing Partner.  To this end, the Implementing Partner shall: a)	put in place an appropriate security plan and maintain the security plan, taking into account the security situation in the country where the project is being carried; b)	assume all risks and liabilities related to the Implementing Partner&#x2019;s security, and the full implementation of the security plan.</narrative>
   </condition>
  </conditions>
  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">% of MPs Trained debating  on Sector Specific issues</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2019">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">60% of MPs in the new Parliament Session new and require induction on SI and parliamentary issues</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">30% of Bills on the Legislative Agenda passed into law</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">25% (2 out 8) recommendations submitted by the Portfolio Committee on Justice, Legal and Parliamentary Affairs on the Constitutional Amendment Bill following national public hearings were adopted by the Executive Further 6 out of 8 Bills passed by Parliament in 2020 PSP targeted Committees were supported by the PSP. These included Veterans of the Liberation Struggle Act, Attorney Generals Amendment Act, and National Prosecuting Authority Act. The PSP supported through legislative analysis skills enhancement contributing to informed debates in the house as well as improved conducting of public hearings by the Committee chairpersons</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">7 Bills passed into law with at least 10% of recommendations from the public factored in the laws 

4 Oversight reports tabled in Parliament.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">As of June 2022 four (4) bills out of the expected 7 bills have been passed into law. These are the Marriages Act, the Insurance, Pensions and Provident Act, the Guardianship of Minors Amendment Act and the Complaints Commission Act. All the laws passed were subjected to public hearings and some recommendations from the public were taken on board by the executive and reflected in the final laws passed. Most recommendation from the public were reflected in the Marriages Bill.
As of June 2022, two oversight reports on the status of prisons and inquiry on sexual harassment in education institutions had been tabled in Parliament by the relevant Committees</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">7 Bills passed into law 
10 % of recommendations for Bill Amendments from Committees incorporated into law by the executive
 
Capacity development of staff and MPs 
 Gender mainstreaming</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">The target of 7 laws out of a 2021 target of passing 7 bills into law was achieved.
 These laws include: Two Bills on the Constitutional Amendment Number 1 and 2 Act, Centre for Education, Innovation, Research and Development Amendment Act, and the Data Protection Act (Source Parliament of Zimbabwe, Parliament Acts Register (2021), These laws are part of the alignment of laws to the Constitution and international human rights instruments as well as contribution towards the national vision of desiring Zimbabwe to be a prosperous and empowered
middle-income economy by 2030.

3 Public Hearings were held on the Guardianship of Minors Amendment Bill H. B. 7, 2020.The target of 1% was reached and surpassed .An average 31% of recommendations for amendments to Bills from Committee Bills were adopted into legislative processes in 2021. These adopted recommendations in include; 10% one (1) out of ten recommendations, from the public consultation report of the Zimbabwe Independent Complaints Commission Bill where the Minister
