In pursuit of Parliament being proactive in its legislative and oversight mandates and as part of capacity development of staff and MPs,  40 staff of Parliament and MPs being members of the Parliament Legal Committee-tasked with the role of checking the constitutionality of laws were exposed to  Post Legislative Analysis skills resulting in the review of effectiveness in terms of contribution to the national development agenda and compliance with the constitution and international frameworks 
(a) the Maintenance of Peace Act [Chapter 11:17];
(b) the Freedom of Information Act [Chapter 10:33]; and
(c) the Electoral Act [Chapter 2:13]; and
A plan of action was developed identifying gaps in the laws and recommending for amendments and strategies for effectiveness and enforcement of the laws 

Despite Covid 19 pandemic restrictions, all bills passed were subjected to public scrutiny through virtual and physical public hearings as well as through social media- face book, radio, and TV programmes. Furthermore , the public was engaged in the first ever budge budget consultations feedback in April and in October in pre-budget consultations- contributing to SDG based budgeting. For instance,1079 citizens (37% being female) attended the first of its kind post budget
feedback meetings for the 2021 national budget and 2027 citizens (45% being female and 2% being persons with disability)
attended the 2022 National Budget Public hearing consultations. Furthermore 11 public hearing meetings throughout the country specifically on one bill ,the Zimbabwe Independent Complaints Commission the that were attended by
1008 citizens (56%being female) , demonstrating some results acheived in 2021
October 2021, some of these hearings were held in very remote areas to ensure that those usually left behind were also included in the budget making process.
 
